WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:09.755
These two proteins were modeled in NanoCoFold. Let's install the
platform, run a public example, and find the result again in the
database.

2
00:00:09.955 --> 00:00:21.047
Start on Ubuntu. Run nvidia-smi in a terminal and check that it lists
your GPU. The installation guide lists the driver, memory, and disk
requirements.

3
00:00:21.247 --> 00:00:30.505
Open the public NanoCoFold repository on GitHub. The English and Korean
guides link to the same release and example inputs.

4
00:00:30.705 --> 00:00:40.922
Download the release with Git or extract the source ZIP. Open a terminal
inside the source folder. You should see install.sh and start.sh.

5
00:00:41.122 --> 00:00:51.088
For the first protein complex example, run the complex installation
profile. It prepares Boltz-2 and Chai-1. We'll use Boltz-2 for this
example.

6
00:00:51.288 --> 00:01:01.088
To follow all four analysis modes, use the all profile instead. Choose
one installation command. Allow time for the engine and model downloads.

7
00:01:01.288 --> 00:01:10.047
Wait for the installation-complete message before starting the server.
This saved installation log shows what completion looks like.

8
00:01:10.247 --> 00:01:18.713
Start the web server in the same folder. Keep the terminal open and open
the exact NanoCoFold address it prints in your browser.

9
00:01:18.913 --> 00:01:27.838
On your first visit, choose your language. Open Environment and setup,
select protein complex prediction, and start the environment check.

10
00:01:28.038 --> 00:01:34.755
When the required checks pass, click Prepare analysis. You can now enter
your proteins and start a prediction.

11
00:01:34.955 --> 00:01:46.630
Load the public example. It contains one copy each of Barnase and
Barstar. For your own FASTA, enter a greater-than sign and a name,
followed by the sequence on the next line.

12
00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:58.338
On the review screen, check for Boltz-2, no MSA, seed 42, and one
planned model. If Run analysis is disabled, return to the environment
check for that engine.

13
00:01:58.538 --> 00:02:10.130
Here the Run analysis button is demonstrated in the existing-nanobody
CPU practice mode. Click once. In Jobs and logs, select your analysis to
see its progress.

14
00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:20.505
This saved GPU run completed one complex model in about 57 seconds.
Check the success and failure counts, then click Open results.

15
00:02:20.705 --> 00:02:29.838
The two input sequences produced one model. Rotate it to see the two
colored chains and inspect where the model places them relative to each
other.

16
00:02:30.038 --> 00:02:41.630
The rows and columns of the PAE plot are residues. Use the color scale
to read the predicted position error. Look across the chain boundary to
assess the relative placement.

17
00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:57.297
In this model, whole-complex ipTM is 0.078, and mean interface PAE is
24.84 angstroms. Those values indicate low confidence in the relative
positions of the chains.

18
00:02:57.497 --> 00:03:08.713
Choose Design new nanobodies and load the public IL-6 example. This mode
starts with a target sequence or structure and generates candidate
nanobody sequences.

19
00:03:08.913 --> 00:03:22.005
The small preset requests one backbone from each generator. Two
candidates evaluated by four engines can give eight models. Public MSA
requires consent to send the example sequences.

20
00:03:22.205 --> 00:03:31.505
This saved run evaluated eight models from two candidate sequences. All
eight models finished. Neither candidate passed final selection.

21
00:03:31.705 --> 00:03:41.880
Open section four to read the exclusions by criterion. One model can
fall below more than one threshold, so a model can appear under several
reasons.

22
00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:52.172
To compare nanobodies you already have, enter the target and the full
VHH sequence for each candidate. Give each candidate a unique FASTA
name.

23
00:03:52.372 --> 00:04:04.172
Four engines evaluated the same candidate. Select an engine to inspect
its result. For Boltz-2, the interface score, ipSAE, was 0.378.

24
00:04:04.372 --> 00:04:13.297
Rotate the structure and inspect the predicted contacts alongside the
interface scores. Binding and affinity are measured experimentally.

25
00:04:13.497 --> 00:04:24.797
For variant evaluation, enter the target and the original nanobody
sequence. The original sequence, labeled WT for wild type, is the
reference for the comparison.

26
00:04:24.997 --> 00:04:34.255
In this example, set the evaluation limit to one variant and include the
wild type. With four engines, the plan shows eight models in total.

27
00:04:34.455 --> 00:04:46.213
The report compares the wild type with N100R. At position one hundred,
the variant has arginine in place of asparagine. Four engines evaluated
both sequences.

28
00:04:46.413 --> 00:05:00.130
Under the same Boltz-2 settings, ipSAE was 0.378 for the wild type and
0.013 for the variant. An affinity change would require experimental
measurement.

29
00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:12.630
Completed web runs appear in the results database automatically. To add
an earlier run, open Explore results, enter its folder inside the
workspace, and click Open existing result.

30
00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:23.838
Enter part of the analysis name or ID, then click Search. Here,
public-complex finds one match. Click the result row to open its report
in a new tab.

31
00:05:24.038 --> 00:05:34.505
Under Explore results, choose Shareable report ZIP and click Export. You
can also download candidate tables or individual structure files from
the same menu.

32
00:05:34.705 --> 00:05:47.213
Extract the entire ZIP and open report.html in your browser. A
collaborator can now explore the same structures and results offline
without installing NanoCoFold.
